["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>You will see far more moose sign than moose. Read it right and it tells you who was here, how long ago, and where to sit tomorrow.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 296\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Six kinds of moose sign. A browse line 1.8 to 2.4 metres high. Snapped stems that a moose bent down to reach the tips. Bark stripped from willow and aspen in winter. Rut pits 45 to 105 centimetres long, soaked with urine. Antler rubs on saplings in fall. Beds, big ovals, often near water.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"296\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Moose sign: 6 to look for</text><rect x=\"12\" y=\"40\" width=\"184\" height=\"76\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Browse line</text><text x=\"104\" y=\"94\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1.8 to 2.4 m high</text><rect x=\"204\" y=\"40\" width=\"184\" height=\"76\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"296\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Snapped stems</text><text x=\"296\" y=\"94\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">bent down to reach</text><rect x=\"12\" y=\"124\" width=\"184\" height=\"76\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"154\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Bark stripped</text><text x=\"104\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">willow, aspen, winter</text><rect x=\"204\" y=\"124\" width=\"184\" height=\"76\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"296\" y=\"154\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rut pit</text><text x=\"296\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">45 to 105 cm, urine</text><rect x=\"12\" y=\"208\" width=\"184\" height=\"76\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"238\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Antler rub</text><text x=\"104\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">saplings, fall</text><rect x=\"204\" y=\"208\" width=\"184\" height=\"76\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"296\" y=\"238\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Bed</text><text x=\"296\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">big oval, near water</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Tracks, droppings, browse, bark, rubs, rut pits, beds (65 to 90%).</li><li>Fresh sign beats old sign every time (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what this chapter covers</summary><ul><li>Tracks to scale, droppings by season, browse and bark, rubs and rut pits, beds, how fresh it is, and how to follow a moose.</li><li>Previous: <a href=\"#/s/mc-moose-07\">Chapter 7, Where they are</a>. Next: <a href=\"#/s/mc-moose-09\">Chapter 9, How to hunt them</a>.</li><li>Deep dives: <a href=\"#/s/tracks\">tracks</a>, <a href=\"#/s/sign\">reading sign</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rs-droppings\">droppings</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rs-beds\">beds</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rs-track-aging\">aging sign</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rs-track-following\">following a track</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 305\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A moose track beside a mule deer track at the same scale. The moose track is about 13 to 15 centimetres long, a long pointed split heart, with dewclaws showing behind in mud or snow. The mule deer track is about 6 to 8 centimetres.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"305\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Track: a giant deer print</text><g transform=\"translate(150 160)\"><path d=\"M-22 -60 Q-30 0 -16 50 Q-6 58 -2 40 Q-4 -10 -6 -62 Q-14 -70 -22 -60 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><path d=\"M22 -60 Q30 0 16 50 Q6 58 2 40 Q4 -10 6 -62 Q14 -70 22 -60 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><ellipse cx=\"-20\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"6\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" opacity=\".7\"/><ellipse cx=\"20\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"6\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" opacity=\".7\"/></g><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"98\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"212\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">13 to 15 cm</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Moose</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">dewclaws show in mud, snow</text><g transform=\"translate(300 170)\"><path d=\"M-12 -30 Q-16 0 -8 26 Q-2 30 0 20 Q-2 -4 -3 -30 Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><path d=\"M12 -30 Q16 0 8 26 Q2 30 0 20 Q2 -4 3 -30 Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/></g><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"196\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"338\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">6 to 8 cm</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mule deer</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"298\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Drawn to the same scale</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>About 13 to 15 cm long: twice a deer track <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Long pointed toes; dewclaws in mud and snow <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-moose-tracks-mud.jpg\" alt=\"Moose tracks in mud: long pointed toes.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Moose tracks in mud: long pointed toes. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_Tracks_on_Seedskadee_National_Wildlife_Refuge_(22388750875).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: tracks in detail</summary><ul><li>App track tables: moose about 11 to 18 cm long, 10 to 15 cm wide, a long pointed heart, often with dewclaws (70%, <a href=\"#/s/tracks\">tracks</a>); about 13 to 15 cm, more than twice a deer track (75%, <a href=\"#/s/habitat-big-game\">habitat tour</a>).</li><li>Maine Inland Fisheries and Wildlife track card: adult moose 4.5 to 5.5 in (about 11 to 14 cm); adult white tail 2.5 to 3 in (85%, via <a href=\"#/s/rs-track-measuring\">measuring tracks</a>).</li><li>Elk tracks are smaller and rounder <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>. A big track by a beaver pond is most likely moose <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Dewclaws show when the moose sinks in: soft mud, snow, or when it runs (70%, Tip).</li><li>Bull or cow from a track alone: not reliable (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 320\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Hoof tracks drawn to the same scale. Deer: pointed heart, about 4 to 10 cm long. Elk: rounder with blunt tips, 10 to 13 cm. Moose: long pointed heart, 11 to 18 cm, often with two dewclaw dots behind. Cow: round, blunt and wide, with no point.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"320\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hoof tracks, same scale</text>\n  <line x1=\"10\" y1=\"215\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"215\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <g fill=\"#8b5a2b\">\n    <g transform=\"translate(55 180) scale(1.6)\"><path d=\"M-2 -20 C-9 -16 -13 -2 -12 12 C-11 19 -3 20 -2 14 Z\"/><path d=\"M2 -20 C9 -16 13 -2 12 12 C11 19 3 20 2 14 Z\"/></g>\n    <g transform=\"translate(150 162) scale(2.4,2.2)\"><path d=\"M-2 -20 C-11 -18 -14 -2 -13 12 C-12 19 -3 20 -2 14 Z\"/><path d=\"M2 -20 C11 -18 14 -2 13 12 C12 19 3 20 2 14 Z\"/></g>\n    <g transform=\"translate(250 140) scale(2.6,3.4)\"><path d=\"M-2 -20 C-9 -16 -13 -2 -12 12 C-11 19 -3 20 -2 14 Z\"/><path d=\"M2 -20 C9 -16 13 -2 12 12 C11 19 3 20 2 14 Z\"/></g>\n    <circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"207\" r=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"262\" cy=\"207\" r=\"4\"/>\n    <g transform=\"translate(345 170)\"><path d=\"M-3 -24 C-22 -22 -26 6 -22 18 C-18 26 -4 26 -3 18 Z\"/><path d=\"M3 -24 C22 -22 26 6 22 18 C18 26 4 26 3 18 Z\"/></g>\n  </g>\n  <g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n    <text x=\"55\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Deer</text>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Elk</text>\n    <text x=\"250\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Moose</text>\n    <text x=\"345\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Cow</text>\n    <text x=\"55\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\">4 to 10 cm</text>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\">10 to 13 cm</text>\n    <text x=\"250\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\">11 to 18 cm</text>\n    <text x=\"345\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\">no point</text>\n    <text x=\"55\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">pointed</text>\n    <text x=\"150\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">blunt tips</text>\n    <text x=\"250\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">dewclaws</text>\n    <text x=\"345\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">round, wide</text>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"312\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Front track length. Pointed end = the way it went</text>\n</svg>\n</figure>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-moose-trail-snow.jpg\" alt=\"A moose trail: a trough with deep holes.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A moose trail: a trough with deep holes. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS staff, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_Browse_Collection,_Yukon-Charley_Rivers,_2003_2_(1482c7be-3b1f-42da-844e-544383bbd5da).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Moose step high: the trail is a trough with deep holes (Tip).</li><li>Snow ages a track by the crust and crumbs <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: snow trails in detail</summary><ul><li>Moose step over deep snow and logs; the trail is a trough with deep holes, not a neat line of prints (Tip, <a href=\"#/s/habitat-big-game\">habitat tour</a>).</li><li>Loose crumbs that fall apart when poked: fresh; disturbed snow bonds into a crust within hours (75%, <a href=\"#/s/snow-tracking\">snow tracking</a>).</li><li>Your November season may have early snow on the plateaus: a gift for reading fresh moose sign (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 280\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Moose droppings. In fall and winter: piles of oval pellets about 2.5 to 4 centimetres long that look like pressed sawdust. In summer: soft clumps or patties from green food. Small deer pellets shown for scale.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"280\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Droppings: winter and summer</text><ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"110\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" stroke=\"#23261f\"/><ellipse cx=\"92\" cy=\"110\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" stroke=\"#23261f\"/><ellipse cx=\"114\" cy=\"110\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" stroke=\"#23261f\"/><ellipse cx=\"136\" cy=\"110\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" stroke=\"#23261f\"/><ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"126\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" stroke=\"#23261f\"/><ellipse cx=\"92\" cy=\"126\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" stroke=\"#23261f\"/><ellipse cx=\"114\" cy=\"126\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" stroke=\"#23261f\"/><ellipse cx=\"136\" cy=\"126\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" stroke=\"#23261f\"/><ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"142\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" stroke=\"#23261f\"/><ellipse cx=\"92\" cy=\"142\" rx=\"10\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" stroke=\"#23261f\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Fall and winter</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">oval pellets, 2.5 to 4 cm</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"204\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">like pressed sawdust</text><path d=\"M260 120 Q300 90 340 120 Q350 140 300 146 Q250 140 260 120 Z\" fill=\"#5a4026\" stroke=\"#23261f\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Summer</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">soft clumps, patties</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"204\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">green food, wet</text><ellipse cx=\"170\" cy=\"250\" rx=\"4\" ry=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><ellipse cx=\"180\" cy=\"250\" rx=\"4\" ry=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><ellipse cx=\"190\" cy=\"250\" rx=\"4\" ry=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><ellipse cx=\"200\" cy=\"250\" rx=\"4\" ry=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"255\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\">deer pellets for scale</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Fall and winter: big oval pellets, like pressed sawdust <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Summer: soft clumps or patties <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: droppings in detail</summary><ul><li>App sign table: moose pellets about 1.5 to 2 cm across and 2.5 to 4 cm long; winter pellets look like pressed sawdust; summer droppings are loose patties (65%, <a href=\"#/s/sign\">reading sign</a>).</li><li>Habitat tour: big oval pellets about 2.5 to 3 cm long, in large piles <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Elk pellets are smaller and often more rounded or dimpled; deer pellets much smaller (65%, <a href=\"#/s/rs-droppings\">droppings</a>).</li><li>In November expect a mix: pellets as twigs take over the diet (Tip).</li><li>Many piles in one small area: a feeding or bedding spot used for days (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Pellets drawn to scale against a centimetre ruler. Deer: oval pellets about 1 to 1.5 cm long. Elk: rounder, 1 to 1.5 cm across, often dimpled on one end. Moose: big, 1.5 to 2 cm across and 2.5 to 4 cm long. Snowshoe hare: slightly flat round balls about 1.2 cm.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Pellets to scale</text>\n  <g fill=\"#5c3b1c\">\n    <ellipse cx=\"45\" cy=\"100\" rx=\"9\" ry=\"14\"/><ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"95\" rx=\"9\" ry=\"14\" transform=\"rotate(20 70 95)\"/><ellipse cx=\"58\" cy=\"125\" rx=\"9\" ry=\"14\" transform=\"rotate(-30 58 125)\"/><ellipse cx=\"82\" cy=\"125\" rx=\"9\" ry=\"13\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"100\" r=\"13\"/><circle cx=\"178\" cy=\"104\" r=\"13\"/><circle cx=\"162\" cy=\"128\" r=\"13\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"250\" cy=\"100\" rx=\"13\" ry=\"22\"/><ellipse cx=\"282\" cy=\"112\" rx=\"13\" ry=\"21\" transform=\"rotate(25 282 112)\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"350\" cy=\"105\" rx=\"9\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#a0784a\"/><ellipse cx=\"368\" cy=\"122\" rx=\"9\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"#a0784a\"/>\n  </g>\n  <g fill=\"#8b5a2b\"><path d=\"M146 92 q4 4 8 0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M174 96 q4 4 8 0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/></g>\n  <g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n    <text x=\"62\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Deer</text>\n    <text x=\"162\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Elk</text>\n    <text x=\"265\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Moose</text>\n    <text x=\"358\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Hare</text>\n    <text x=\"62\" y=\"194\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">oval</text><text x=\"62\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">1 to 1.5 cm</text>\n    <text x=\"162\" y=\"194\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">round, dimple</text><text x=\"162\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">1 to 1.5 cm</text>\n    <text x=\"265\" y=\"194\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">big oval</text><text x=\"265\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">2.5 to 4 cm</text>\n    <text x=\"358\" y=\"194\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">flat ball</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">1.2 cm</text>\n  </g>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"235\" width=\"360\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#f3d36b\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n  <g stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n    <path d=\"M38 235 v14 M56 235 v8 M74 235 v14 M92 235 v8 M110 235 v14 M128 235 v8 M146 235 v14 M164 235 v8 M182 235 v14\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"254\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">ruler: 1 tick = 1 cm</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"288\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Summer on green feed: soft clumps or patties</text>\n</svg>\n</figure>","<figure class=\"diagram anim\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Animation. A moose stands by a tall willow. It reaches up to about 2.5 m, then bends and breaks the stem down with its jaws to get the twig tips that were out of reach. Final label: breaks branches to reach the tips. Look for snapped stems in willow and aspen.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"19\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Moose ride down tall twigs</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"250\" width=\"400\" height=\"50\" rx=\"0\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g transform=\"translate(150 252) scale(1.6)\"><path d=\"M-20 -30 Q -18 -46 0 -46 Q 14 -50 16 -40 L 18 -30 Q 10 -22 -20 -24 Z\" fill=\"#4a3322\"/><path d=\"M14 -42 L28 -40 L30 -32 L24 -30 L16 -34 Z\" fill=\"#4a3322\"/><path d=\"M-16 -26 v26 M-10 -26 v26 M10 -28 v28 M15 -28 v28\" stroke=\"#4a3322\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M20 -42 q-6 -10 4 -12 q2 6 -2 10 M22 -42 q8 -9 12 -6\" stroke=\"#8b6b45\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\"/></g><g><path d=\"M260 252 Q 262 160 258 70\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M238.0 80 q 2.0 -22.0 12.0 -17.6 q 8.0 -11.0 16.0 -2.2 q 12.0 -4.4 12.0 19.8 Z\" fill=\"#7da35a\"/><animateTransform attributeName=\"transform\" type=\"rotate\" values=\"0 260 252;0 260 252;-38 260 252\" keyTimes=\"0;0.4;1\" dur=\"4s\" begin=\"0.5s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></g><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"20\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/><text x=\"26\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">2.5 m reach</text><g opacity=\"0\"><rect x=\"230\" y=\"50\" width=\"160\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Breaks branches</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">to reach the tips</text><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"4.5s\" dur=\"0.5s\" fill=\"freeze\"/></g><text x=\"200\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Look for snapped stems in willow and aspen</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>A moose breaks the stem down to reach the tips. <button class=\"anim-replay\">↻ Replay</button></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Twigs bitten off up to 2.5 m; stems snapped down <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Bark stripped from willow and poplar in winter <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-moose-willows.jpg\" alt=\"Willow tips bitten off at chest height.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Willow tips bitten off at chest height. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park from Yellowstone NP, USA, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cow_moose_browsing_on_willows_(15951276174).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-aspen-grove-snow.jpg\" alt=\"Aspen: look for teeth marks and stripped bark.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Aspen: look for teeth marks and stripped bark. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jason Hollinger, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aspen_Grove_in_Snow_(2276157824).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: browse in detail</summary><ul><li>BC account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>: moose reach twigs up to 2.5 m, \"chew off large diameter twigs, and often break down branches with their jaws to get at tips that are out of reach\". They \"strip bark from willows and poplars in winter\".</li><li>Browse line: moose 1.8 to 2.4 m; elk 1.5 to 1.8 m; deer to about 1.2 m (65%, <a href=\"#/s/sign\">reading sign</a>). Alaska reports moose browse lines 6 to 8 ft (1.8 to 2.4 m) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Moose and deer have no upper front teeth: they tear twigs, leaving a ragged end; hares cut clean at 45 degrees (85%, <a href=\"#/s/sign\">reading sign</a>).</li><li>Broken, bent willow stems with fresh, pale wood: a moose fed here recently (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 320\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Browse line heights. Below the line every twig is eaten. Deer reach to about 1.2 m. A line at 1.5 to 1.8 m says elk. 1.8 to 2.4 m says moose. A person 1.8 m tall stands for scale.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"320\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Browse line: who reached?</text>\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"290\" width=\"400\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n  <g stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"><path d=\"M40 290 V50\"/><path d=\"M34 290 h12 M34 230 h12 M34 170 h12 M34 110 h12 M34 50 h12\"/></g>\n  <g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\"><text x=\"30\" y=\"295\">0</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"235\">0.6</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"175\">1.2</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"115\">1.8</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"55\">2.4</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"38\">m</text></g>\n  <rect x=\"80\" y=\"170\" width=\"70\" height=\"120\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" opacity=\".85\"/>\n  <rect x=\"170\" y=\"110\" width=\"70\" height=\"180\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" opacity=\".65\"/>\n  <rect x=\"170\" y=\"110\" width=\"70\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".5\"/>\n  <rect x=\"260\" y=\"50\" width=\"70\" height=\"240\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" opacity=\".45\"/>\n  <rect x=\"260\" y=\"50\" width=\"70\" height=\"60\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".5\"/>\n  <g font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"115\" y=\"240\">Deer</text><text x=\"205\" y=\"240\">Elk</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"240\">Moose</text></g>\n  <g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"115\" y=\"162\">to 1.2 m</text><text x=\"205\" y=\"102\">1.5 to 1.8 m</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"44\">1.8 to 2.4 m</text></g>\n  <g fill=\"#2f3a2b\"><circle cx=\"365\" cy=\"122\" r=\"10\"/><rect x=\"355\" y=\"134\" width=\"20\" height=\"80\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"356\" y=\"210\" width=\"7\" height=\"80\"/><rect x=\"367\" y=\"210\" width=\"7\" height=\"80\"/></g>\n  <text x=\"365\" y=\"310\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">You</text>\n</svg>\n</figure>","<figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-bull-side.jpg\" alt=\"Bull moose, side view, in fall. Tall front shoulders, hump, long legs, broad flat antler palms.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bull moose, side view, in fall. Tall front shoulders, hump, long legs, broad flat antler palms. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jacob W. Frank, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alces_alces_1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Rut pits: pawed holes 45 to 105 cm long, soaked with urine <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Rubs on saplings; brush thrashed <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: rut sign in detail</summary><ul><li>Bull moose dig rut pits: about 45 to 105 cm long and 8 to 15 cm deep, soaked with urine, strong smell (65%, <a href=\"#/s/sign\">reading sign</a>).</li><li>BC account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>: cows \"wallow in the urine soaked rutting pits the bulls have made\".</li><li>Bulls rub velvet off \"against trees or shrubs\" in early September <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>, then rub and thrash saplings through the rut <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>In November the pits are old; fresh pits mean a bull still keen, or a late cow (Tip, my reading of the second and third heats).</li><li>A <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Wallow\">Wallow</button> in the app glossary is the elk word; moose sign is the rut pit (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-subalpine-forest.jpg\" alt=\"Subalpine forest of spruce and lodgepole pine, Manning Park. Spruce grouse country.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Subalpine forest of spruce and lodgepole pine, Manning Park. Spruce grouse country. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Tim Gage at Flickr, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Picea_engelmannii_Pinus_contorta.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Big flattened ovals, often alone, in willow or timber near water <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Size: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> (no source found).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: beds in detail</summary><ul><li>App: moose beds are bigger than elk or deer beds and often alone, in willow or timber near water (60%, <a href=\"#/s/sign\">reading sign</a>).</li><li>Melted snow beds in winter; flattened grass or leaves in fall (Tip, <a href=\"#/s/rs-beds\">beds</a>).</li><li>Droppings next to a bed are common; moose often defecate on rising (Tip).</li><li>A bed still warm, or with crisp edges in fresh snow: the moose is very close. Stop, glass, check the wind (Tip).</li><li>Do not walk through a bedding area you plan to hunt: leave it quiet (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Freshness ladder for moose sign. Hours old: pellets wet and shiny, browsed twig ends white and wet. One to two days: pellets dull, ends drying. Days to weeks: pellets dry, ends grey. Old: pellets pale and crumbly. Weather changes everything; a guide, not a clock.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">How fresh is it?</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"44\" width=\"14\" height=\"46\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Hours</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"84\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">pellets wet, shiny; browse ends white, wet</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"100\" width=\"14\" height=\"46\" fill=\"#f0a070\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">1 to 2 days</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">pellets dull; ends drying</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"156\" width=\"14\" height=\"46\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"176\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Days to weeks</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">pellets dry; ends grey</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"212\" width=\"14\" height=\"46\" fill=\"#bbbbbb\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Old</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">pellets pale, crumbly</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Weather changes everything: a guide, not a clock</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Wet and shiny pellets: hours (Tip).</li><li>Browse ends still pale and wet: today or yesterday (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: aging sign in detail</summary><ul><li>Freshness signs (Tip, <a href=\"#/s/rs-track-aging\">aging sign</a>): wet shiny pellets; crisp track edges; loose crumbs in snow; pale wet wood on browsed twigs; sap on broken stems; urine smell at a rut pit.</li><li>Weather changes everything: rain, frost and sun age sign fast or slow. Treat any time estimate as a guide (75%, <a href=\"#/s/rs-track-aging\">aging sign</a>).</li><li>Old sign is still useful: it shows where moose winter or feed year after year (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram anim\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Animation: the hunter walks slowly beside the blood trail, not on it, and flags each blood spot. When the blood stops, go back to the last flag. Never wander ahead.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23261f\">Walk beside the blood, look ahead</text><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"230\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/><circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"200\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"196\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/><circle cx=\"190\" cy=\"170\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/><circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"160\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/><circle cx=\"290\" cy=\"130\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#b3261e\"/><path id=\"sk-trail-beside-p\" d=\"M30 262 C39.0 256.7, 66.3 236.0, 84.0 230.0 C101.7 224.0, 119.0 231.0, 136.0 226.0 C153.0 221.0, 169.3 206.0, 186.0 200.0 C202.7 194.0, 218.7 196.3, 236.0 190.0 C253.3 183.7, 279.3 167.0, 290.0 162.0 C300.7 157.0, 298.3 160.3, 300.0 160.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 5\"/><g opacity=\"0\"><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"0.6s\" dur=\"0.3s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"230\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M40 210 L52 215 L40 220 Z\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></g><g opacity=\"0\"><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"1.6s\" dur=\"0.3s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"180\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M90 180 L102 185 L90 190 Z\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></g><g opacity=\"0\"><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"2.6s\" dur=\"0.3s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"196\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"176\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M140 176 L152 181 L140 186 Z\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></g><g opacity=\"0\"><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"3.6s\" dur=\"0.3s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M190 150 L202 155 L190 160 Z\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></g><g opacity=\"0\"><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"4.6s\" dur=\"0.3s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><line x1=\"240\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M240 140 L252 145 L240 150 Z\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></g><g opacity=\"0\"><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"5.6s\" dur=\"0.3s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><line x1=\"290\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M290 110 L302 115 L290 120 Z\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></g><g><circle r=\"9\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><animateMotion dur=\"5.6s\" fill=\"freeze\" calcMode=\"linear\"><mpath href=\"#sk-trail-beside-p\"/></animateMotion></g><g opacity=\"0\"><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"5.8s\" dur=\"0.5s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"104\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b3261e\">Blood stops?</text></g><g opacity=\"0\"><animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;1\" begin=\"6.6s\" dur=\"0.5s\" fill=\"freeze\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Back to the last flag. Never wander ahead.</text></g><text x=\"20\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\">Flag every spot</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">as you pass it</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>Walk beside the trail, not on it. <button class=\"anim-replay\">↻ Replay</button></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Walk beside the trail; glass ahead every few steps (Tip).</li><li>Wind in your face; the moose may be bedded close (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: following in detail</summary><ul><li>Following a track (Tip, <a href=\"#/s/rs-track-following\">following a track</a>): mark the last good print; walk beside the trail; look 50 to 100 m ahead more than at your feet; stop often.</li><li>Moose often loop back and bed facing their back trail or with the wind behind them (Tip, hunter consensus). Swing wide and downwind of the line if you think it bedded.</li><li>On a blood trail after a shot, wait first (chapter 10).</li><li>Your partner on the track, you ahead and to the side with the rifle ready: works well (Tip, <a href=\"#/s/hunting-partner\">partner</a>).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 305\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A moose track beside a mule deer track at the same scale. The moose track is about 13 to 15 centimetres long, a long pointed split heart, with dewclaws showing behind in mud or snow. The mule deer track is about 6 to 8 centimetres.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"305\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Track: a giant deer print</text><g transform=\"translate(150 160)\"><path d=\"M-22 -60 Q-30 0 -16 50 Q-6 58 -2 40 Q-4 -10 -6 -62 Q-14 -70 -22 -60 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><path d=\"M22 -60 Q30 0 16 50 Q6 58 2 40 Q4 -10 6 -62 Q14 -70 22 -60 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><ellipse cx=\"-20\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"6\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" opacity=\".7\"/><ellipse cx=\"20\" cy=\"78\" rx=\"6\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" opacity=\".7\"/></g><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"98\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"212\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">13 to 15 cm</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Moose</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">dewclaws show in mud, snow</text><g transform=\"translate(300 170)\"><path d=\"M-12 -30 Q-16 0 -8 26 Q-2 30 0 20 Q-2 -4 -3 -30 Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><path d=\"M12 -30 Q16 0 8 26 Q2 30 0 20 Q2 -4 3 -30 Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/></g><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"196\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"338\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">6 to 8 cm</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mule deer</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"298\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Drawn to the same scale</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li><strong>Rhyme:</strong> \"Big as your hand, twice a deer; dewclaws down, a moose was here.\"</li><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 13 to 15 cm track; 1.8 to 2.4 m browse line (65 to 75%).</li><li><strong>Old hand's rule</strong> (tradition): \"Fresh sign, sit tight. Old sign, keep walking.\"</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: more hooks</summary><ul><li><strong>Rhyme:</strong> \"Pellets in winter, patties in June; pale twig ends: he left here soon.\"</li><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 45 to 105: rut pit length in cm <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li><strong>Old hand's rule</strong> (opinion): \"The bed is the moose's front porch. Do not walk across it.\"</li></ul><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Calling every big track a moose: check size and shape.</li><li>Walking straight down the moose's trail.</li><li>Judging bull or cow from a track.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Take a tape measure and a phone. Measure 3 tracks and 3 pellets at a wetland and photograph them next to the tape. Compare with this chapter.</p></aside></details>","<ul><li>BC Ministry of Environment, Lands and Parks (2000), <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/moose.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\"Moose in British Columbia\"</a>, read directly, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span></li><li>Alaska Department of Fish and Game, <a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/static/education/wns/moose.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moose notebook</a>, read directly, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Maine Inland Fisheries and Wildlife, <a href=\"https://www.maine.gov/IFW/docs/MDIFW-Animal-Tracking-Card-color.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">animal tracking card</a>, via <a href=\"#/s/rs-track-measuring\">measuring tracks</a> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Hunt Mentor sessions with their own sources: <a href=\"#/s/tracks\">tracks</a>, <a href=\"#/s/sign\">reading sign</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rs-droppings\">droppings</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rs-beds\">beds</a>, <a href=\"#/s/rs-track-aging\">aging sign</a>, <a href=\"#/s/snow-tracking\">snow tracking</a>, <a href=\"#/s/habitat-big-game\">habitat tour</a> (60 to 85% as marked there)</li><li>Research notes: <code>research/2026-10-11-masterclass-moose.md</code>.</li></ul>"]